Community-Acquired Pneumonia
Respiratory System
Fever/rigors, Pleuritic chest pain, Focal lobar consolidation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-011
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of high fever, rigors, and severe right-sided chest pain on inspiration, necessitating immediate medical review to prevent sepsis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a rapid deterioration over 12 hours with rigors, localized chest pain, and tachypnea. Urgent assessment was clinically mandated to evaluate for sepsis and initiate broad-spectrum antibiotics, as delaying until the next in-hours period posed a high risk of systemic decompensation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I started shaking uncontrollably this morning, and now it feels like a sharp stitch in my ribs every time I try to breathe in.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began abruptly 12 hours ago with profound fatigue, myalgias, and sudden onset of fever with rigors and chills. Over the past 6 hours, the patient developed a productive cough with rust-colored sputum. They describe sharp, pleuritic chest pain localized strictly to the right hemithorax. Dyspnea is progressive and proportional to the perceived restriction from the pain.
Functional Status: Previously fully independent; currently bedbound due to severe fatigue and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Advanced age and rapid functional decline increase the risk of falls and dehydration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 39.2 C | RR: 24 breaths/min | SpO2: 92% on room air | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Toxic appearance, diaphoretic, flushed, and visibly splinting the right side of the chest during respiration.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals focal crackles and bronchial breath sounds over the right lower lobe. Dullness to percussion and increased tactile fremitus are prominent over the same area, strongly indicating focal lobar consolidation. Cardiovascular exam confirms sinus tachycardia.
Environmental Observations: Patient is lying flat; multiple blankets used despite high core temperature.
Medication Reconciliation: Only over-the-counter paracetamol taken with minimal antipyretic effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Community-Acquired Pneumonia - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of high fever, rigors, and rust-colored purulent sputum.

Acute Bronchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of focal consolidation signs, tachypnea, and high systemic toxicity.

Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is strictly pleuritic and respiratory symptoms predominate.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with a classic tetrad of sudden high fever, rigors, pleuritic chest pain, and clinical signs of focal lobar consolidation. The presence of tachycardia, tachypnea, and borderline hypoxia indicates significant physiological stress and borderline sepsis criteria. Delaying clinical assessment and antibiotic therapy until the next available in-hours appointment would predictably lead to profound sepsis, respiratory failure, and mandatory ICU admission. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to confirm the lobar origin, administer the first dose of targeted systemic antibiotics immediately, and provide aggressive symptomatic relief.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Amoxicillin 1g and Clarithromycin 500mg immediately. Prescribed a 7-day course. Provided Ibuprofen 400mg for pleuritic pain and antipyresis.
Positive Behavioral Change: Drink one full glass of water immediately after every severe coughing fit; achieving this precise hydration target thins the purulent mucus, making it physically easier to cough up and directly reducing the pain of dry heaving.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required at this stage.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 48 hours to ensure resolution of fever and clinical improvement.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 if confusion develops, breathing becomes rapid at rest, or if unable to keep fluids down.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; a chest X-ray will be required to formally document the resolution of the consolidation once stabilized.
Clinical Course: Fevers and rigors should improve within 48 hours of starting antibiotics; the cough and fatigue may persist for several weeks.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you feel dizzy upon standing, if your lips turn blue, or if you begin to feel confused or disoriented.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The antibiotics will directly kill the bacteria in your right lung, and the anti-inflammatories will stop the sharp pain, allowing you to breathe deeply and clear the infection.
Actionable Steps: Take the pain relief exactly every 6 hours; preventing the sharp chest pain allows you to take deep breaths, which physically forces the infected mucus out of the lung bases.

